KG42.register("nodes-589616ec371fc0bb72a9ef4d",{"id":"KG40-3bf0b2932a5782b5","code":"PRD014.2","label":"Electricity product with source-category restrictions on use, substitution and storage","definition":"Electric energy commodity distinguished by source attributes, whose categories directly restrict the permitted use and the scope for substitution and storage as grid electricity; the permissions of each category are retained separately.","layer":"L2","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"product.local_energy","concept_ids":["product.local_energy"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Economic object: heterogeneous electric energy with categorical identity","Delivery permission: the permitted use scope corresponding to the category","Substitution boundary: the grid-electricity substitution and storage permissions corresponding to the category","Quantity dimension: electric energy; no equivalence conversion is presupposed between categories"]},"exclusions":[["Each unit of electric energy is classified according to the relevant source attributes; attributes may involve local source, generation technology, and preferred subscribers.","The permitted-use set for the agent is K_i, and the permitted-substitution set is K_g.","When k∈K_g, b_ikg≥0; when k∉K_g, b_ikg=0.","Cases include grid energy, green energy and subsidised energy.","In the case, green energy is permitted to be substituted, stored, or sold as grid electricity; the use of subsidised energy is limited to low-income households.","The three-category setup and the five-minute wholesale trading conducted through the platform are only case conditions."]],"members":[{"id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::V::product.local_energy","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","market_meaning":"Each unit of energy is assigned an energy class according to relevant source attributes; energy is treated as a heterogeneous product based on source attributes valued by prosumers. Scope: Energy within the distribution network served by the proposed P2P platform. The prosumer may use the energy class to supply its loads. Scope: Energy-class use for supplying each prosumer's loads. Condition: The energy class belongs to that prosumer's allowed load-supply class set. The energy class may be substituted for and stored as grid energy. Scope: Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy in the multiclass platform. Condition: The energy class belongs to the designated permitted substitution/storage subset. The amount of that energy class stored as grid energy must be zero. Scope: Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy at every interval of the MPC time horizon. Condition: The energy class is outside the permitted substitution/storage subset. The case study defines three energy classes based on prosumer preferences: grid energy, green energy and subsidised energy. Scope: IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study with 55 prosumers. Green energy may be substituted for and stored or sold as grid energy. Scope: Green energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study. Condition: Green energy is traded at a premium in this case study. Only low-income households may use subsidised energy. Scope: Subsidised energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Energy within the distribution network served by the proposed P2P platform. | Energy-class use for supplying each prosumer's loads. | Green energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study. | IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study with 55 prosumers. | Subsidised energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study. | Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy at every interval of the MPC time horizon. | Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy in the multiclass platform.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E20","v141-b-G01-E24","v141-b-G01-E25","v141-b-g01-E06","v141-b-g02-e027","v141-b-g03-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C002","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C003","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C005","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C006","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C007"],"decision_anchor":"product.local_energy","source_rule_sha256":"a5782eaa60dbe38e55598c4fe99ab9282a8da0e70bdaf0922e48196941392d7f","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010","node_id":"product.local_energy","function":"product.local_energy","unit_id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-a-M01::product.local_energy","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Principle of Operation","anchor_text":"M. ences. For example: r Prosumers may prefer to obtain the best ﬁnancial return, ws regardless of t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"It is assumed the prosumers have individual energy preferences. For example: Prosumers may prefer to obtain the best financial return, regardless of the source/destination of their energy. Prosumers may prefer to obtain energy from local sources or particular generation technologies. Prosumers may prefer to trade energy with particular subscribers (e.g., friends/relatives, low-income residents, community organisations). To account for the prosumers’ heterogeneous preferences, each unit of energy in the distribution network is assigned an ‘energy class’ relating to relevant attributes of its source. The prosumer preferences are incorporated into the proposed P2P market platform through utility functions (envisaged as being set by users), giving their additional willingness to pay to obtain, or to supply, energy with these attributes. Prosumers wishing only to obtain the best financial return would set their utility functions for each class of energy to zero.","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"It is assumed the prosumers have individual energy preferences. For example: Prosumers may prefer to obtain the best financial return, regardless of the source/destination of their energy. Prosumers may prefer to obtain energy from local sources or particular generation technologies. Prosumers may prefer to trade energy with particular subscribers (e.g., friends/relatives, low-income residents, community organisations). To account for the prosumers’ heterogeneous preferences, each unit of energy in the distribution network is assigned an ‘energy class’ relating to relevant attributes of its source. The prosumer preferences are incorporated into the proposed P2P market platform through utility functions (envisaged as being set by users), giving their additional willingness to pay to obtain, or to supply, energy with these attributes. Prosumers wishing only to obtain the best financial return would set their utility functions for each class of energy to zero."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"VI. Conclusion","anchor_text":"product, based on attributes of its source that are perceived by prosumers to have value. This has b","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"The new concept of multi-class energy management has been presented, which allows energy to be treated as a heterogeneous product, based on attributes of its source that are perceived by prosumers to have value.","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The new concept of multi-class energy management has been presented, which allows energy to be treated as a heterogeneous product, based on attributes of its source that are perceived by prosumers to have value."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 reserved for low-income household","equation_id":"(7)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Also, each prosumer, i \\in \\mathcal{N}, has load constraints, renewable generation constraints and battery constraints. The prosumer load constraints require that the total power allocated to its load meets its load predictions, z_i^{pred(t)}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. Let \\mathcal{K}_i \\subseteq \\mathcal{K} be the set of energy classes that prosumer i is allowed to use to supply its loads (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, subsidised energy is reserved for low-income households). \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{K}_i} z_{ik}^{(t)} + z_{ig}^{(t)} = z_i^{pred(t)}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. \\quad 0 \\le z_{ig}^{(t)}, 0 \\le z_{ik}^{(t)}, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}.","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"Also, each prosumer, i \\in \\mathcal{N}, has load constraints, renewable generation constraints and battery constraints. The prosumer load constraints require that the total power allocated to its load meets its load predictions, z_i^{pred(t)}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. Let \\mathcal{K}_i \\subseteq \\mathcal{K} be the set of energy classes that prosumer i is allowed to use to supply its loads (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, subsidised energy is reserved for low-income households). \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{K}_i} z_{ik}^{(t)} + z_{ig}^{(t)} = z_i^{pred(t)}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. \\quad 0 \\le z_{ig}^{(t)}, 0 \\le z_{ik}^{(t)}, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 reserved for low-income household","equation_id":"(7)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The prosumer load constraints require that the total power allocated to its load meets its load predictions, \\(z_i^{pred(t)}\\), \\(\\forall t\\in\\mathcal T\\). Let \\(\\mathcal K_i\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\) be the set of energy classes that prosumer \\(i\\) is allowed to use to supply its loads (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, subsidised energy is reserved for low-income households). \\[ \\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K_i} z_{ik}^{(t)} + z_{ig}^{(t)} = z_i^{pred(t)}, \\quad \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{7} \\] \\[ 0\\le z_{ig}^{(t)},\\quad 0\\le z_{ik}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{8} \\]","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The prosumer load constraints require that the total power allocated to its load meets its load predictions, \\(z_i^{pred(t)}\\), \\(\\forall t\\in\\mathcal T\\). Let \\(\\mathcal K_i\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\) be the set of energy classes that prosumer \\(i\\) is allowed to use to supply its loads (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, subsidised energy is reserved for low-income households). \\[ \\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K_i} z_{ik}^{(t)} + z_{ig}^{(t)} = z_i^{pred(t)}, \\quad \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{7} \\] \\[ 0\\le z_{ig}^{(t)},\\quad 0\\le z_{ik}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{8} \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since nega- tive SoC levels are not allowed. tch the","equation_id":"(13)-(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since negative SoC levels are not allowed. \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ik}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}(\\mathbf{b}_{ik} + \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}), \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ig}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}\\left(\\mathbf{b}_{ig} - \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{K}} \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}\\right), \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\text{ is a vector with } |\\mathcal{T}| \\text{ elements, all equal to 0}. In (13), (14), \\mathbf{b}_{ikg} = [b_{ikg}^{(t_0)} \\cdots b_{ikg}^{(T)}]^T, where b_{ikg}^{(t)}, t \\in \\mathcal{T} is the amount of class k energy stored as grid energy. Certain energy classes, k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g \\subseteq \\mathcal{K}, are allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, green energy is traded at a premium and therefore may be substituted for grid energy).","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The following additional constraints are needed since negative SoC levels are not allowed. \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ik}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}(\\mathbf{b}_{ik} + \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}), \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ig}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}\\left(\\mathbf{b}_{ig} - \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{K}} \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}\\right), \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\text{ is a vector with } |\\mathcal{T}| \\text{ elements, all equal to 0}. In (13), (14), \\mathbf{b}_{ikg} = [b_{ikg}^{(t_0)} \\cdots b_{ikg}^{(T)}]^T, where b_{ikg}^{(t)}, t \\in \\mathcal{T} is the amount of class k energy stored as grid energy. Certain energy classes, k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g \\subseteq \\mathcal{K}, are allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, green energy is traded at a premium and therefore may be substituted for grid energy)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"MORSTYN AND MCCULLOCH: MULTICLASS ENERGY MANAGEMENT FOR PEER-TO-P energy classes that can be substit","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. 0 \\le b_{ikg}^{(t)}, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad b_{ikg}^{(t)} = 0, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K} \\setminus \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}.","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. 0 \\le b_{ikg}^{(t)}, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad b_{ikg}^{(t)} = 0, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K} \\setminus \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e009","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since nega- tive SoC levels are not allowed. tch the","equation_id":"(13)-(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since negative SoC levels are not allowed. \\[ \\mathbf 0 \\le s_{ik}^{(t_0)}\\mathbf 1 + A_{bi}(\\mathbf b_{ik}+\\mathbf b_{ikg}),\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T, \\tag{13} \\] \\[ \\mathbf 0 \\le s_{ig}^{(t_0)}\\mathbf 1 + A_{bi}\\left(\\mathbf b_{ig}-\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}\\mathbf b_{ikg}\\right),\\quad \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{14} \\] \\(\\mathbf 0\\) is a vector with \\(|\\mathcal T|\\) elements, all equal to 0. In (13), (14), \\(\\mathbf b_{ikg}=[b_{ikg}^{(t_0)}\\ \\cdots\\ b_{ikg}^{(T)}]^{\\mathsf T}\\), where \\(b_{ikg}^{(t)}, t\\in\\mathcal T\\) is the amount of class \\(k\\) energy stored as grid energy. Certain energy classes, \\(k\\in\\mathcal K_g\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\), are allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, green energy is traded at a premium and therefore may be substituted for grid energy).","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The following additional constraints are needed since negative SoC levels are not allowed. \\[ \\mathbf 0 \\le s_{ik}^{(t_0)}\\mathbf 1 + A_{bi}(\\mathbf b_{ik}+\\mathbf b_{ikg}),\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T, \\tag{13} \\] \\[ \\mathbf 0 \\le s_{ig}^{(t_0)}\\mathbf 1 + A_{bi}\\left(\\mathbf b_{ig}-\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}\\mathbf b_{ikg}\\right),\\quad \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{14} \\] \\(\\mathbf 0\\) is a vector with \\(|\\mathcal T|\\) elements, all equal to 0. In (13), (14), \\(\\mathbf b_{ikg}=[b_{ikg}^{(t_0)}\\ \\cdots\\ b_{ikg}^{(T)}]^{\\mathsf T}\\), where \\(b_{ikg}^{(t)}, t\\in\\mathcal T\\) is the amount of class \\(k\\) energy stored as grid energy. Certain energy classes, \\(k\\in\\mathcal K_g\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\), are allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, green energy is traded at a premium and therefore may be substituted for grid energy)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e010","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"MORSTYN AND MCCULLOCH: MULTICLASS ENERGY MANAGEMENT FOR PEER-TO-P energy classes that can be substit","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. \\[ 0\\le b_{ikg}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T, \\tag{15} \\] \\[ b_{ikg}^{(t)}=0,\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K\\setminus\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{16} \\]","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. \\[ 0\\le b_{ikg}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T, \\tag{15} \\] \\[ b_{ikg}^{(t)}=0,\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K\\setminus\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{16} \\]"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e027","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Results","anchor_text":"and average resistance between the prosumers and main grid of Rg = 0.084Ω are used. t.) There are th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The operation of the proposed P2P energy market platform based on multi-class energy management was verified using the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder [42], with 55 prosumers. ... There are three types of prosumers in the distribution network, ‘green prosumers’, ‘philanthropic prosumers’ and ‘low-income households’. Based on their preferences, three energy classes are introduced; ‘grid energy’, ‘green energy’ and ‘subsidised energy’. Grid energy, is traded with the wholesale market at five minute trading intervals through the platform agent. Green energy is traded at a premium, so it is allowed to be substituted for and stored/sold as grid energy. Only low-income households are allowed to use subsidised energy.","paper":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"The operation of the proposed P2P energy market platform based on multi-class energy management was verified using the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder [42], with 55 prosumers. ... There are three types of prosumers in the distribution network, ‘green prosumers’, ‘philanthropic prosumers’ and ‘low-income households’. Based on their preferences, three energy classes are introduced; ‘grid energy’, ‘green energy’ and ‘subsidised energy’. Grid energy, is traded with the wholesale market at five minute trading intervals through the platform agent. Green energy is traded at a premium, so it is allowed to be substituted for and stored/sold as grid energy. Only low-income households are allowed to use subsidised energy."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["attribute_differentiated_energy_product"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"KG40-L2-SC-L2-664204a5283e78a0","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L2","ontological_kind":"SC-L2-664204a5283e78a0"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Electric energy commodity distinguished by source attributes, whose categories directly restrict the permitted use and the scope for substitution and storage as grid electricity; the permissions of each category are retained separately.","identity_fields":["Economic object: heterogeneous electric energy with categorical identity","Delivery permission: the permitted use scope corresponding to the category","Substitution boundary: the grid-electricity substitution and storage permissions corresponding to the category","Quantity dimension: electric energy; no equivalence conversion is presupposed between categories"]},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-a-M01::product.local_energy","paper_id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"product.local_energy","source_occurrences":[{"id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::V::product.local_energy","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","market_meaning":"Each unit of energy is assigned an energy class according to relevant source attributes; energy is treated as a heterogeneous product based on source attributes valued by prosumers. Scope: Energy within the distribution network served by the proposed P2P platform. The prosumer may use the energy class to supply its loads. Scope: Energy-class use for supplying each prosumer's loads. Condition: The energy class belongs to that prosumer's allowed load-supply class set. The energy class may be substituted for and stored as grid energy. Scope: Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy in the multiclass platform. Condition: The energy class belongs to the designated permitted substitution/storage subset. The amount of that energy class stored as grid energy must be zero. Scope: Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy at every interval of the MPC time horizon. Condition: The energy class is outside the permitted substitution/storage subset. The case study defines three energy classes based on prosumer preferences: grid energy, green energy and subsidised energy. Scope: IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study with 55 prosumers. Green energy may be substituted for and stored or sold as grid energy. Scope: Green energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study. Condition: Green energy is traded at a premium in this case study. Only low-income households may use subsidised energy. Scope: Subsidised energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Energy within the distribution network served by the proposed P2P platform. | Energy-class use for supplying each prosumer's loads. | Green energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study. | IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study with 55 prosumers. | Subsidised energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study. | Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy at every interval of the MPC time horizon. | Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy in the multiclass platform.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E20","v141-b-G01-E24","v141-b-G01-E25","v141-b-g01-E06","v141-b-g02-e027","v141-b-g03-e01"]},"constraint_refs":["28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C002","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C003","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C005","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C006","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C007"],"decision_anchor":"product.local_energy","source_rule_sha256":"a5782eaa60dbe38e55598c4fe99ab9282a8da0e70bdaf0922e48196941392d7f"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each unit of energy is assigned an energy class according to relevant source attributes; energy is treated as a heterogeneous product based on source attributes valued by prosumers. Scope: Energy within the distribution network served by the proposed P2P platform.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E06","v141-b-g03-e01"],"ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E06","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Principle of Operation","anchor_text":"M. ences. For example: r Prosumers may prefer to obtain the best ﬁnancial return, ws regardless of t","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"It is assumed the prosumers have individual energy preferences. For example: Prosumers may prefer to obtain the best financial return, regardless of the source/destination of their energy. Prosumers may prefer to obtain energy from local sources or particular generation technologies. Prosumers may prefer to trade energy with particular subscribers (e.g., friends/relatives, low-income residents, community organisations). To account for the prosumers’ heterogeneous preferences, each unit of energy in the distribution network is assigned an ‘energy class’ relating to relevant attributes of its source. The prosumer preferences are incorporated into the proposed P2P market platform through utility functions (envisaged as being set by users), giving their additional willingness to pay to obtain, or to supply, energy with these attributes. Prosumers wishing only to obtain the best financial return would set their utility functions for each class of energy to zero."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-activation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"VI. Conclusion","anchor_text":"product, based on attributes of its source that are perceived by prosumers to have value. This has b","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"The new concept of multi-class energy management has been presented, which allows energy to be treated as a heterogeneous product, based on attributes of its source that are perceived by prosumers to have value."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The prosumer may use the energy class to supply its loads. Scope: Energy-class use for supplying each prosumer's loads. Condition: The energy class belongs to that prosumer's allowed load-supply class set.","expression":"\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{K}_i} z_{ik}^{(t)} + z_{ig}^{(t)} = z_i^{\\mathrm{pred}(t)},\\; \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T};\\quad 0\\le z_{ig}^{(t)},\\; 0\\le z_{ik}^{(t)},\\; \\forall k\\in\\mathcal{K},\\; \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T}.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R010-x-zik","v141-c-R010-x-zig"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R010-p-zpred","v141-c-R010-p-Ki"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E20","v141-b-g02-e005"],"ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 reserved for low-income household","equation_id":"(7)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Also, each prosumer, i \\in \\mathcal{N}, has load constraints, renewable generation constraints and battery constraints. The prosumer load constraints require that the total power allocated to its load meets its load predictions, z_i^{pred(t)}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. Let \\mathcal{K}_i \\subseteq \\mathcal{K} be the set of energy classes that prosumer i is allowed to use to supply its loads (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, subsidised energy is reserved for low-income households). \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{K}_i} z_{ik}^{(t)} + z_{ig}^{(t)} = z_i^{pred(t)}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. \\quad 0 \\le z_{ig}^{(t)}, 0 \\le z_{ik}^{(t)}, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 reserved for low-income household","equation_id":"(7)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The prosumer load constraints require that the total power allocated to its load meets its load predictions, \\(z_i^{pred(t)}\\), \\(\\forall t\\in\\mathcal T\\). Let \\(\\mathcal K_i\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\) be the set of energy classes that prosumer \\(i\\) is allowed to use to supply its loads (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, subsidised energy is reserved for low-income households). \\[ \\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K_i} z_{ik}^{(t)} + z_{ig}^{(t)} = z_i^{pred(t)}, \\quad \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{7} \\] \\[ 0\\le z_{ig}^{(t)},\\quad 0\\le z_{ik}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{8} \\]"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The energy class may be substituted for and stored as grid energy. Scope: Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy in the multiclass platform. Condition: The energy class belongs to the designated permitted substitution/storage subset.","expression":"0 \\le b_{ikg}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal{K}_g,\\; \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T}.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R010-x-bikg"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R010-p-Kg"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E24","v141-b-G01-E25","v141-b-g02-e009","v141-b-g02-e010"],"ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since nega- tive SoC levels are not allowed. tch the","equation_id":"(13)-(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since negative SoC levels are not allowed. \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ik}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}(\\mathbf{b}_{ik} + \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}), \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ig}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}\\left(\\mathbf{b}_{ig} - \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{K}} \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}\\right), \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\text{ is a vector with } |\\mathcal{T}| \\text{ elements, all equal to 0}. In (13), (14), \\mathbf{b}_{ikg} = [b_{ikg}^{(t_0)} \\cdots b_{ikg}^{(T)}]^T, where b_{ikg}^{(t)}, t \\in \\mathcal{T} is the amount of class k energy stored as grid energy. Certain energy classes, k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g \\subseteq \\mathcal{K}, are allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, green energy is traded at a premium and therefore may be substituted for grid energy)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"MORSTYN AND MCCULLOCH: MULTICLASS ENERGY MANAGEMENT FOR PEER-TO-P energy classes that can be substit","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. 0 \\le b_{ikg}^{(t)}, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad b_{ikg}^{(t)} = 0, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K} \\setminus \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e009","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since nega- tive SoC levels are not allowed. tch the","equation_id":"(13)-(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since negative SoC levels are not allowed. \\[ \\mathbf 0 \\le s_{ik}^{(t_0)}\\mathbf 1 + A_{bi}(\\mathbf b_{ik}+\\mathbf b_{ikg}),\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T, \\tag{13} \\] \\[ \\mathbf 0 \\le s_{ig}^{(t_0)}\\mathbf 1 + A_{bi}\\left(\\mathbf b_{ig}-\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}\\mathbf b_{ikg}\\right),\\quad \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{14} \\] \\(\\mathbf 0\\) is a vector with \\(|\\mathcal T|\\) elements, all equal to 0. In (13), (14), \\(\\mathbf b_{ikg}=[b_{ikg}^{(t_0)}\\ \\cdots\\ b_{ikg}^{(T)}]^{\\mathsf T}\\), where \\(b_{ikg}^{(t)}, t\\in\\mathcal T\\) is the amount of class \\(k\\) energy stored as grid energy. Certain energy classes, \\(k\\in\\mathcal K_g\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\), are allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, green energy is traded at a premium and therefore may be substituted for grid energy)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e010","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"MORSTYN AND MCCULLOCH: MULTICLASS ENERGY MANAGEMENT FOR PEER-TO-P energy classes that can be substit","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. \\[ 0\\le b_{ikg}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T, \\tag{15} \\] \\[ b_{ikg}^{(t)}=0,\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K\\setminus\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{16} \\]"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The amount of that energy class stored as grid energy must be zero. Scope: Substitution and storage of energy classes as grid energy at every interval of the MPC time horizon. Condition: The energy class is outside the permitted substitution/storage subset.","expression":"b_{ikg}^{(t)} = 0,\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal{K}\\setminus\\mathcal{K}_g,\\; \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T}.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R010-x-bikg"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R010-p-Kg"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E24","v141-b-G01-E25","v141-b-g02-e010"],"ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since nega- tive SoC levels are not allowed. tch the","equation_id":"(13)-(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The following additional constraints are needed since negative SoC levels are not allowed. \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ik}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}(\\mathbf{b}_{ik} + \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}), \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\le s_{ig}^{(t_0)} \\mathbf{1} + A_{bi}\\left(\\mathbf{b}_{ig} - \\sum_{k \\in \\mathcal{K}} \\mathbf{b}_{ikg}\\right), \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}. \\quad \\mathbf{0} \\text{ is a vector with } |\\mathcal{T}| \\text{ elements, all equal to 0}. In (13), (14), \\mathbf{b}_{ikg} = [b_{ikg}^{(t_0)} \\cdots b_{ikg}^{(T)}]^T, where b_{ikg}^{(t)}, t \\in \\mathcal{T} is the amount of class k energy stored as grid energy. Certain energy classes, k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g \\subseteq \\mathcal{K}, are allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy (e.g., in the example from Fig. 1, green energy is traded at a premium and therefore may be substituted for grid energy)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-G01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"MORSTYN AND MCCULLOCH: MULTICLASS ENERGY MANAGEMENT FOR PEER-TO-P energy classes that can be substit","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. 0 \\le b_{ikg}^{(t)}, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}, \\quad b_{ikg}^{(t)} = 0, \\forall k \\in \\mathcal{K} \\setminus \\mathcal{K}_g, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e010","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Multi-class Energy Management","anchor_text":"MORSTYN AND MCCULLOCH: MULTICLASS ENERGY MANAGEMENT FOR PEER-TO-P energy classes that can be substit","equation_id":"(15)-(16)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The following constraints are introduced to restrict the energy classes that can be substituted and stored as grid energy. \\[ 0\\le b_{ikg}^{(t)},\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T, \\tag{15} \\] \\[ b_{ikg}^{(t)}=0,\\quad \\forall k\\in\\mathcal K\\setminus\\mathcal K_g,\\ \\forall t\\in\\mathcal T. \\tag{16} \\]"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The case study defines three energy classes based on prosumer preferences: grid energy, green energy and subsidised energy. Scope: IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study with 55 prosumers.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027"],"ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e027","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Results","anchor_text":"and average resistance between the prosumers and main grid of Rg = 0.084Ω are used. t.) There are th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The operation of the proposed P2P energy market platform based on multi-class energy management was verified using the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder [42], with 55 prosumers. ... There are three types of prosumers in the distribution network, ‘green prosumers’, ‘philanthropic prosumers’ and ‘low-income households’. Based on their preferences, three energy classes are introduced; ‘grid energy’, ‘green energy’ and ‘subsidised energy’. Grid energy, is traded with the wholesale market at five minute trading intervals through the platform agent. Green energy is traded at a premium, so it is allowed to be substituted for and stored/sold as grid energy. Only low-income households are allowed to use subsidised energy."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Green energy may be substituted for and stored or sold as grid energy. Scope: Green energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study. Condition: Green energy is traded at a premium in this case study.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027"],"ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e027","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Results","anchor_text":"and average resistance between the prosumers and main grid of Rg = 0.084Ω are used. t.) There are th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The operation of the proposed P2P energy market platform based on multi-class energy management was verified using the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder [42], with 55 prosumers. ... There are three types of prosumers in the distribution network, ‘green prosumers’, ‘philanthropic prosumers’ and ‘low-income households’. Based on their preferences, three energy classes are introduced; ‘grid energy’, ‘green energy’ and ‘subsidised energy’. Grid energy, is traded with the wholesale market at five minute trading intervals through the platform agent. Green energy is traded at a premium, so it is allowed to be substituted for and stored/sold as grid energy. Only low-income households are allowed to use subsidised energy."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C007","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Only low-income households may use subsidised energy. Scope: Subsidised energy in the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder case study.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["product.local_energy"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e027"],"ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e027","location":{"location_id":"src-96285c344a-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Results","anchor_text":"and average resistance between the prosumers and main grid of Rg = 0.084Ω are used. t.) There are th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"The operation of the proposed P2P energy market platform based on multi-class energy management was verified using the IEEE European Low Voltage Test Feeder [42], with 55 prosumers. ... There are three types of prosumers in the distribution network, ‘green prosumers’, ‘philanthropic prosumers’ and ‘low-income households’. Based on their preferences, three energy classes are introduced; ‘grid energy’, ‘green energy’ and ‘subsidised energy’. Grid energy, is traded with the wholesale market at five minute trading intervals through the platform agent. Green energy is traded at a premium, so it is allowed to be substituted for and stored/sold as grid energy. Only low-income households are allowed to use subsidised energy."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-x-zik","symbol":"z_{ik}^{(t)}","meaning":"Average allocated load power of energy class k supplied to prosumer i at interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real (0 \u003c= z_{ik}^{(t)}) for i in N, k in K, t in T","unit":"power","indices":["i","k","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-G01-E20","v141-b-g02-e005"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::x::v141-c-R010-x-zik"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-x-zig","symbol":"z_{ig}^{(t)}","meaning":"Average allocated load power of grid energy supplied to prosumer i at interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real (0 \u003c= z_{ig}^{(t)}) for i in N, t in T","unit":"power","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-G01-E20","v141-b-g02-e005"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::x::v141-c-R010-x-zig"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R010-x-bikg","symbol":"b_{ikg}^{(t)}","meaning":"Amount of class k energy stored as grid energy at interval t, as used in the battery-output vector.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real for k in K_g; equal to 0 for k in K set-minus K_g, for i in N, t in T","unit":null,"indices":["i","k","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E24","v141-b-G01-E25","v141-b-g02-e009","v141-b-g02-e010"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::x::v141-c-R010-x-bikg"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-p-zpred","symbol":"z_i^{pred(t)}","meaning":"Predicted load power of prosumer i at interval t.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"power","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::theta::v141-c-R010-p-zpred"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-p-Ki","symbol":"\\mathcal{K}_i","meaning":"Set of energy classes that prosumer i is allowed to use to supply its loads.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\mathcal{K}_i \\subseteq \\mathcal{K}"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E20"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::theta::v141-c-R010-p-Ki"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R010-p-Kg","symbol":"\\mathcal{K}_g","meaning":"Set of energy classes allowed to be substituted for and stored as grid energy.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\mathcal{K}_g \\subseteq \\mathcal{K}"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-G01-E24"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","declaration_ref":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::theta::v141-c-R010-p-Kg"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-a-M01::product.local_energy","stage":"L2","subtype":"attribute_differentiated_energy_product","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The traded object is heterogeneous electric energy assigned to categories according to the source attributes valued by prosumers. Category use is constrained by K_i; the boundary of substitution and storage as grid electricity is defined by K_g; case products include grid energy, green energy and subsidised energy.","form_kind":"conceptual","identity_fields":["Object: electric energy with source attributes","Core relation: each unit of energy is assigned to an energy category according to the relevant source attributes","Product boundary: categories have permitted use and the scope of grid-electricity substitution and storage"],"variation_fields":["Source attributes may involve local source, generation technology, and preferred subscribers","The permitted-use set K_i and the permitted-substitution set K_g of the agent","When k∈K_g, b_ikg≥0; when k∉K_g, b_ikg=0.","In the case, green energy is permitted to be substituted, stored, or sold as grid electricity","In the case, the use scope of subsidised energy is limited to low-income households","The three-category setup and the five-minute wholesale trading conducted through the platform belong to case conditions"],"constraint_refs":["28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C001","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C002","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C003","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C005","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C006","28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C007"],"paper_id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"product.local_energy","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-a-M01::product.local_energy","retained_variants":["Each unit of electric energy is classified according to the relevant source attributes; attributes may involve local source, generation technology, and preferred subscribers.","The permitted-use set for the agent is K_i, and the permitted-substitution set is K_g.","When k∈K_g, b_ikg≥0; when k∉K_g, b_ikg=0.","Cases include grid energy, green energy and subsidised energy.","In the case, green energy is permitted to be substituted, stored, or sold as grid electricity; the use of subsidised energy is limited to low-income households.","The three-category setup and the five-minute wholesale trading conducted through the platform are only case conditions."],"evidence_refs":["28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-a-M01::product.local_energy::projection"]}],"functional":{"is_child":true,"subdivision_pending":false,"baseline_parent_id":"COMP-863167ac5ca0c411","baseline_parent_code":"PRD014","secondary_class":"SC-L2-664204a5283e78a0","category":{"id":"SC-L2-664204a5283e78a0","stage":"L2","label_zh":"Electric energy with category-specific use and substitution permissions","definition_zh":"Electric energy commodity divided by source attributes, whose commodity category directly determines permitted use and the permission to substitute or store as other electric energy categories; category permissions are retained separately, and free substitution or equivalent conversion is not presupposed.","main_identity_axes":["Economic object: heterogeneous electric energy with categorical identity","Delivery permission: the category restricts the permitted user or use","Substitution permission: the category restricts the scope of substitution to other electric energy categories and storage","Quantity dimension: electric energy; category conversion does not by default imply quantity conversion"],"inclusion_criteria":["The traded object is electric energy, and the commodity category directly determines use, substitution, or storage permissions.","Source attributes participate in the definition of product categories and permissions, not merely as generation-source annotations."],"exclusion_criteria":["Electric energy that merely marks equipment, source, surplus-electricity eligibility, or delivery period, without forming category permissions.","Standalone certificates, quotas, capacity rights, and non-electric energy products.","Products that take active power itself as the clearing object."],"supporting_unit_ids":["28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-a-M01::product.local_energy"],"status":"agent_defined_structurally_validated"},"definition":{"definition_zh":"Electric energy commodity distinguished by source attributes, whose categories directly restrict the permitted use and the scope for substitution and storage as grid electricity; the permissions of each category are retained separately.","identity_fields":["Economic object: heterogeneous electric energy with categorical identity","Delivery permission: the permitted use scope corresponding to the category","Substitution boundary: the grid-electricity substitution and storage permissions corresponding to the category","Quantity dimension: electric energy; no equivalence conversion is presupposed between categories"],"member_unit_ids":["28-multiclass_prosumer_preferences::v141-a-M01::product.local_energy"]}},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/166"});
